+The first important question is in what range we want to consider cell type interactions. We provide a generalisation of a guideline for the maximum radius of @Baddeley2015-book-point-patterns. This guideline is to not consider any radii beyond $1/2$ of the bounding-circle radius of an image as else effects at the border of a field-of-view become too extreme. The histogram of all bounding-circle radii across all images and $1/2$ of the smallest bin of this histogram as a heuristic are shown below. 
+
+```{r, rMaxHeuristic, results="hide", message = FALSE, warning = FALSE, fig.width=8, fig.height=4}
+p <- rMaxHeuristic(spe,
+    subsetby="image_number", marks="cell_type"
+)
+p
+```
+
+We will consider a radius of $50 \mu m$ to be a) below the heurstic of $91 \mu m$ defined above and consider shorter range interactions in our data. With a cell diameter of $\sim 10 \mu m$ the `rMax` of $50 \mu m$ results in interactions across 5 cell pairs. 
+
+Apart from the radius we have to define some other parameters as well. Important are: `selection` to define the cell types to consider (if `NULL` it will compare all), `fun` to specify the spatial function, `marks` to specify the cell type annotation in the `colData`, `correction` to specify which border correction to compare in the functional model, `transformation` if a variance-stabilising transformation shall be applied. The parameters `sample_id, image_id, condition` are important for the statistical inference. All other parameters can be specified to achieve better results but there are defaults specified as well. 
+
+```{r, spatialFDA-calc, results="hide", message = FALSE, warning = FALSE}
+#relevel to have non-diabetic as the reference category
+spe$patient_stage <- relevel(factor(spe$patient_stage), "Non-diabetic")
+
+#run the spatial statistics inference
+resLs <- crossSpatialInference(
+    spe, 
+    selection=c("alpha", "Tc", "Th"),
     fun="Gcross", 
-    marks="cell_type", 
